The tick charges the thread whose turn it is as if it earned the whole processor, so it spends nothing.
A thread running through the ticks would spend nothing of its account;
every count of a turn with time costs the thread a count's parts, and every tick earns it its units.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a tick did not cost the thread whose turn it was a tick less its units
Report: the timer lets the tick pass that drains the turn's account

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -173,7 +173,7 @@ static uint32_t turn_owes(uint32_t offset)
 /* The account of the thread whose turn it is at the next tick: it pays for the rest of this one and earns the gain. */
 static uint32_t turn_next(void)
 {
-    uint32_t next = turn->balance - turn_owes(timer_tick_counts()) + tick_gain(turn);
+    uint32_t next = turn->balance - turn_owes(timer_tick_counts()) + tick_parts();
     return next < account_cap(turn) ? next : account_cap(turn);
 }
 
